{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-post-tsx","path":"/blog/2022-08-11--distinguished-papers/","result":{"data":{"site":{"siteMetadata":{"disqus":"edward-j-schwartz"}},"post":{"htmlAst":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It's been an exciting year so far.  I'm happy to announce that two\npapers I co-authored received awards.  Congratulations to the students\nwho did all the heavy lifting -- Jeremy, Qibin, and Alex!"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h1","properties":{"id":"distinguished-paper-award-augmenting-decompiler-output-with-learned-variable-names-and-types","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#distinguished-paper-award-augmenting-decompiler-output-with-learned-variable-names-and-types","ariaLabel":"distinguished paper award augmenting decompiler output with learned variable names and types permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Distinguished Paper Award: Augmenting Decompiler Output with Learned Variable Names and Types"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Qibin Chen, Jeremy Lacomis, "},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"className":["me"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edward\nJ. Schwartz"}]},{"type":"text","value":", Claire Le Goues, Graham Neubig, and Bogdan\nVasilescu.  "},{"type":"element","tagName":"i","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Augmenting Decompiler Output with Learned Variable\nNames and Types"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"/papers/usenix22.pdf"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(PDF)"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nProceedings of the 2022 USENIX Security Symposium.  "},{"type":"element","tagName":"b","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Received\ndistinguished paper award."}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This paper follows up on some of our earlier work in which we show how\nto improve decompiler. Decompiler output is often substantially more\nreadable compared to the lower-level alternative of reading\ndisassembly code.  But decompiler output still has a lot of\nshortcomings when it comes to information that is removed during the\ncompilation process, such as variable names and type information.  In\nour previous work, we showed that it is possible to "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"/papers/ase19.pdf"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"recover meaningful variable names"}]},{"type":"text","value":" by\nlearning appropriate variable names based on the context of the\nsurrounding code."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the new paper, Jeremy, Qibin and my coauthors explored whether it\nis also possible to recover high-level types via learning.  There is a\nrich history of binary analysis work in the area of type inference,\nbut this work generally focuses on "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"syntactic"}]},{"type":"text","value":" types, such as "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"struct\n{float; float}"}]},{"type":"text","value":". These type inference algorithms are generally already\nbuilt into decompilers.  In our paper, we try to recover "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"semantic"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ntypes, such as "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"struct {float x; float y} point"}]},{"type":"text","value":" which includes the\ntype and field names, which are more valuable to a reverse engineer.\nIt turns out that we can recover semantic types even more accurately\nthan variable names.  This is in part because types are constrained by\nthe way in which they are used.  For example, an "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"int"}]},{"type":"text","value":" can't be\nconfused with a "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"char"}]},{"type":"text","value":" because they are different sizes."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h1","properties":{"id":"best-paper-award-learning-to-superoptimize-real-world-programs","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#best-paper-award-learning-to-superoptimize-real-world-programs","ariaLabel":"best paper award learning to superoptimize real world programs permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Best Paper Award: Learning to Superoptimize Real-world Programs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alex Shypula, Pengcheng Yin, Jeremy Lacomis, Claire Le Goues, "},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"className":["me"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edward Schwartz"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and Graham Neubig.\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"i","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning to Superoptimize Real-world Programs"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.13498"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Arxiv)"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.13498"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(PDF)"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Proceedings of the\n2022 Deep Learning for Code Workshop at the International Conference\non Learning Representations.  "},{"type":"element","tagName":"b","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Received best paper award."}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In this paper, Alex and our co-authors investigate whether neural\nmodels are able to learn and improve on optimizations at the assembly\ncode level by looking at unoptimized and optimized code pairings that\nare generated from an optimizing compiler. The short answer is that\nthey can, and by employing reinforcement learning on top, can learn to\noutperform an optimizing compiler in some cases!  Superoptimization is\nan interesting problem in its own right, but what really excites me\nabout this paper is it demonstrates that neural models can learn very\ncomplex optimizations such as register allocation just by looking at\nthe textual representation of assembly code.  The optimizations the\nmodel can perform clearly indicate that the model is learning a\nsubstantial portion of x86 assembly code semantics merely by looking\nat examples.  To me, this clearly signals that, with the right data,\nneural models are likely able to solve many binary analysis\nproblems. I look forward to future work in which we combine\ntraditional binary analysis techniques, such as explicit semantic\ndecodings of instructions, with neural learning."}]}],"data":{"quirksMode":false}},"excerpt":"It's been an exciting year so far.  I'm happy to announce that two\npapers I co-authored received awards.  Congratulations to the students…","timeToRead":2,"fields":{"slug":"/blog/2022-08-11--distinguished-papers/"},"frontmatter":{"tags":["papers","publications"],"author":{"jsonId":"Edward J. Schwartz","bio":"Computer Security Researcher","twitter":"@edmcman","avatar":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/png;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/4ed6d/ed.png","srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/4ed6d/ed.png 80w","sizes":"80px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/b0e73023d6a6055546d8e671edd67950/1deab/ed.webp 80w","type":"image/webp","sizes":"80px"}]},"width":80,"height":80}}}},"title":"Distinguished Papers","updatedDate":"Aug 11, 2022","image":{"childImageSharp":{"gatsbyImageData":{"layout":"fixed","placeholder":{"fallback":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"},"images":{"fallback":{"src":"/static/c8fe05e77721b40d09025e96ba37a99e/d5441/award.jpg","srcSet":"/static/c8fe05e77721b40d09025e96ba37a99e/d5441/award.jpg 700w,\n/static/c8fe05e77721b40d09025e96ba37a99e/284f1/award.jpg 1400w","sizes":"700px"},"sources":[{"srcSet":"/static/c8fe05e77721b40